WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.120
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.790
What you see here is a bunch of vector images.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:12.860
Vector images are not like most images that
you see on the internet, those are raster

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:13.860
images.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:19.090
Those are like photos, and are made of pixels,
while vector images are not made of pixels,

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.500
they are made of shapes.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.150
These vector images have lots of advantages,
for instance, they have really small file

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:31.580
sizes, can be zoomed into as much as we desire
and things don’t get pixelated.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:37.199
And hence, vector images are really well suited
for logos, maps, user interface icons, and

00:00:37.199 --> 00:00:38.199
more.

00:00:38.199 --> 00:00:44.570
Now, if we wish to, we can convert vector
images into raster images, so the shapes will

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:49.199
become pixels, this is easy, but here is the
problem.

00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:51.690
If we do it once, there is no going back.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.269
Or at least, not easily.

00:00:53.269 --> 00:00:58.910
This method promises to make this conversion
a two-way street, so we can take a raster

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:04.640
image, a photo if you will, and work with
it as if it were a vector image.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.380
Now what does that mean?

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.780
Oh boy, a lot of goodies.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:14.020
For instance, we can perform sculpting, or
in other words, manipulating shapes without

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.470
touching any pixels.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.640
We can work with the shapes here instead.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:20.640
Much easier.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.480
Or, my favorite, perform painterly rendering.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.600
Now what you see here is not the new algorithm
performing this.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:33.640
This is a genetic algorithm I wrote a few
years ago that takes a target image, which

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:40.090
is the Mona Lisa here, takes a bunch of randomly
colored triangles and starts reorganizing

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:44.460
them to get as close to the target image as
possible.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.380
The source code and a video explaining how
it works is available in the video description.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.860
Now, let’s see how this new method performs
on a similar task.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:58.390
Oh yeah, it can start with a large number
of different shapes, and just look at how

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:03.980
beautifully these shapes evolve and start
converging to the target image.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:04.980
Loving it.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.780
But that’s not all.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:12.340
It also has a nice solution to an old, but
challenging problem in computer graphics that

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.290
is referred to as seam carving.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:18.870
If you ask me, I like to call it image squishing.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.040
Why?

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.340
Well, look here.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:29.239
This gives us an easy way of intelligently
squishing an image into different aspect ratios.

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:30.769
So good.

00:02:30.769 --> 00:02:34.540
So can we measure how well it does what it
does?

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:38.780
How does it compare to, for instance, Adobe’s
state of the art method when vectorizing a

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:39.890
photo?

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:45.599
Well, it can not only do more, but it also
does it better.

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:51.049
The new method is significantly closer to
the target image here, no question about it.

00:02:51.049 --> 00:02:56.950
And now comes the best part: it not only provides
higher-quality results than the previous methods,

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:02.810
but it only takes approximately a second to
perform all this.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:03.940
Wow.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:10.109
So there you go, finally, with this new technique,
we can edit pixels as if they weren’t pixels

00:03:10.109 --> 00:03:11.109
at all.

00:03:11.109 --> 00:03:14.749
It feels like we a living in a science fiction
world.

00:03:14.749 --> 00:04:00.989
What a time to 
be alive!

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:09.989
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

